# Paths vibecheck skips when scanning its own repository.
#
# Everything here is insecure on purpose. Nothing here is a suppression of a
# real problem — if you find yourself adding a line to hide a genuine finding,
# fix the finding instead.

# The guides and the landing page quote vulnerable code so that readers can
# recognise it in their own projects. Flagging our own worked examples would
# be a long joke at our expense.
guides/
index.html

# The research writeup quotes the same code the guides do — it names
# dangerouslyAllowBrowser in prose, and .html counts as JavaScript to the
# extension gate, so the article about the finding becomes the finding.
posts/

# tests/ used to be listed here. It no longer needs to be: findings in test
# paths are demoted to info automatically, because nothing in a test file
# ships. One fewer suppression to trust.

# The security rules are a table of vulnerability descriptions, so their prose
# matches their own patterns: "subprocess is called with shell=True" is a fix
# prompt, not a subprocess call. Only this one data file is excluded (it moved
# here from vibecheck/rules.py); every module with logic in it is still
# scanned, including the alias left at the old path.
vibecheck/checks/security/rules/__init__.py

# The integrity check's pattern tables name the SDKs, endpoints and paths it
# looks for, so they would be evidence of themselves. Data only; the logic in
# signals.py is still scanned.
vibecheck/checks/integrity/patterns.py
